// @emit-error emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape
// FALSE-REJECT xfail — and the pin for the SECOND compiler crash in this family.
//
// A declared struct produced by a CALL, bound to a union-annotated const. Sound program,
// refused. The reject is the gap; what this case guards is that refusing is what happens.
//
// `structIdxMatchesVariantIdx` bounds-checked `ssi < 0` and neither upper bound. `emitFail`
// RECORDS the first failure and returns -1 without stopping the pass, so after the reject below
// was already recorded, emission continued with a row index that never reached the field
// columns — `sFieldCount[ssi]` read past the array and TRAPPED the compiler. The diagnostic it
// had already recorded never printed; the user saw a wasm backtrace.
//
// The binding spelling is a DIFFERENT path from the argument spelling
// (`xfail-false-reject-call-result-union-arg.vl`, the `emitUnionBoxArg` twin test): fixing that
// one left this one still trapping, which is why both are pinned. Neither needs same-shape
// unions and neither needs `is` — #1611 filed the family as "same-shape union crash" and the
// bisect found the trigger is a CALL RESULT IN A UNION POSITION.
//
// EXPECTED eventually: compiles and prints 200. ACTUAL: the reject pinned above — a diagnostic,
// which is the part this case defends.
//
// WHERE THE REJECT COMES FROM — measured, three layers down, none of it fixed here.
//
//   1. The failing valtype is `kind=struct` with an index NO STRUCT ROW BACKS. Instrumented,
//      the emitter reports `idx=0 rows=0 heap=0 vars=1`: the struct table is EMPTY and the
//      variant table has the arm.
//
//   2. That is BY DESIGN. `collectS` skips the `TypeDecl` when `variantIndexOf` claims it — "a
//      union variant `TypeDecl` is owned by the variant table, NOT a standalone struct". So a
//      declared struct used as a union arm has no struct row, and a value of that type must
//      take the VARIANT heap type.
//
//   3. The kind ladder already tries variant first (`retVariantFlag` before `retTRFlag`), but
//      `retVariantFlag` reads the CHECKER's `cVariantMemberTyIxs`, which does not cover this
//      shape — so the kind falls through to "struct" and the valtype emitter is handed an index
//      no struct row backs.
//
//   4. ASKING THE EMITTER'S OWN VARIANT TABLE DOES NOT FIX IT EITHER. An arm consulting
//      `variantRowOfTy` (the type-keyed `uVarTyIx` column) ahead of the struct arm was built and
//      MEASURED TO NEVER FIRE: `uVarTyIx` does not carry this type. The variant row exists by
//      NAME while both type-keyed indexes miss it — the same gap B167 measured in the struct
//      table's D0 type column, in the variant table.
//
// So the next attempt is not a new predicate at the kind ladder; all three that exist have been
// tried. It is the variant table's TYPE column, which is populated for fewer rows than the name
// column — fix that and the kind ladder answers on its own.
//
// B184 CORRECTS TWO OF THE LAYERS ABOVE, and adds a fourth dead end. All measured.
//
//   - The kind does NOT come from the ladder's struct arm. That arm is NEVER REACHED for these
//     programs (probed). It comes from `annRepKindOf` — the rep descriptor, consulted FIRST,
//     which answers "struct": structurally true, and the wrong TABLE.
//
//   - The variant table's TYPE column is NOT missing this type. `declTyIxOfName` resolves at
//     both push sites (probed, never fired), and `uVarTyIx.length` is 1 when the kind is
//     decided. The earlier `variantRowOfTy` arm never fired because it sat BELOW the
//     descriptor's early return — placement, not coverage.
//
//   - FLIPPING THE KIND DOES NOT WORK. Moving that arm ABOVE the descriptor, gated on "the
//     variant table holds this type and the struct table does not", makes 7 of 10 repros
//     COMPILE — into INVALID MODULES:
//
//         vl check --codegen  =>  Found 0 errors
//         vl run              =>  Invalid input WebAssembly code at offset 189:
//                                 type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)
//
//     Two distinct heap types with the same printed name: the signature now says variant while
//     the body still BUILDS the plain struct. Strictly worse than the reject — the same trade
//     `xfail-false-reject-inline-same-shape-array-union.vl` records for its own second attempt.
//
// So the classification is not the gap. A real fix has to change what the value IS — either the
// arm gets a struct row, or the producer builds the variant — not merely what it is called.
//
// B185 — A FIFTH DEAD END, and the neighbour that works. All measured.
//
//   - GIVING THE ARM A STRUCT ROW makes all ten repros compile AND RUN CORRECTLY. It also
//     breaks `types/variant-as-value.vl` into an INVALID MODULE, and that fixture pins the
//     opposite design on purpose: a concrete variant used as a first-class value "rides the
//     variant struct `(ref $(uVarIdx + vi))` directly (kind 8)". A struct row makes the same
//     value ride the plain struct instead, and the two heap types are distinct.
//
//     Lifting the skip outright is worse still: 49 corpus files move, three regress, one of
//     them invalid. Narrowing the row to arms that appear as a FuncDecl RETURN type is what
//     leaves exactly the one conflict above — which is not a tuning problem, it is the design
//     saying a union arm's values belong to the variant table.
//
//   - THE MACHINERY ALREADY EXISTS AND WORKS, for the DECLARED-union spelling in ARGUMENT
//     position: `type U = A | i32` with `f(mkA())` compiles and runs on master today. The
//     construction is context-driven (`pendingVariantIdx` builds a bare variant in variant
//     context). What these xfails need is for that path to cover the INLINE-union arm, not a
//     new rep and not a new row.
//
//   - THE BINDING SPELLING OF THAT SAME DECLARED UNION IS A SEPARATE, WORSE BUG — filed as
//     issue #1678: `const b: U = mkB()` passes `vl check --codegen` and emits an INVALID
//     MODULE. Found while probing this; not part of this family, and higher severity than the
//     rejects here.
type A = { tag: i32, v: i32 }
type B = { tag: i32, v: i32 }
function mkB(): B { return { tag: 2, v: 200 } }
const b: A | B = mkB()
print(b.v)
